T* (BOOK III. \(_Duodecimo_\), CHAPTER 1. \(_Huzza Porpoise_\).?This is the) Tj
T* (common porpoise found almost all over the globe. The name is of my own) Tj
T* (bestowal; for there are more than one sort of porpoises, and something) Tj
T* (must be done to distinguish them. I call him thus, because he always) Tj
T* (swims in hilarious shoals, which upon the broad sea keep tossing) Tj
T* (themselves to heaven like caps in a Fourth-of-July crowd. Their) Tj
T* (appearance is generally hailed with delight by the mariner. Full of) Tj
T* (fine spirits, they invariably come from the breezy billows to windward.) Tj
T* (They are the lads that always live before the wind. They are accounted) Tj
T* (a lucky omen. If you yourself can withstand three cheers at beholding) Tj
T* (these vivacious fish, then heaven help ye; the spirit of godly) Tj
T* (gamesomeness is not in ye. A well-fed, plump Huzza Porpoise will yield) Tj
T* (you one good gallon of good oil. But the fine and delicate fluid) Tj
T* (extracted from his jaws is exceedingly valuable. It is in request among) Tj
T* (jewellers and watchmakers. Sailors put it on their hones. Porpoise meat) Tj
T* (is good eating, you know. It may never have occurred to you that a) Tj
T* (porpoise spouts. Indeed, his spout is so small that it is not very) Tj
T* (readily discernible. But the next time you have a chance, watch him;) Tj
T* (and you will then see the great Sperm whale himself in miniature.) Tj
T* () Tj
